
944 Problem:-

You are given an array of n strings strs, all of the same length.
The strings can be arranged such that there is one on each line, making a grid.
For example, strs = ["abc", "bce", "cae"] can be arranged as follows:
abc
bce
cae
You want to delete the columns that are not sorted lexicographically. In the above example (0-indexed), columns 0 ('a', 'b', 'c') and 2 ('c', 'e', 'e') are sorted, while column 1 ('b', 'c', 'a') is not, so you would delete column 1.
Return the number of columns that you will delete.

Example 1:-
Input: strs = ["cba","daf","ghi"]
Output: 1
Explanation: The grid looks as follows:
  cba
  daf
  ghi
Columns 0 and 2 are sorted, but column 1 is not, so you only need to delete 1 column.

Example 2:-
Input: strs = ["a","b"]
Output: 0
Explanation: The grid looks as follows:
  a
  b
Column 0 is the only column and is sorted, so you will not delete any columns.

Example 3:-
Input: strs = ["zyx","wvu","tsr"]
Output: 3
Explanation: The grid looks as follows:
  zyx
  wvu
  tsr
All 3 columns are not sorted, so you will delete all 3.
 
Constraints:-
n == strs.length
1 <= n <= 100
1 <= strs[i].length <= 1000
strs[i] consists of lowercase English letters.

=========================================================================================================================================================================================

Solution:-

Intuition:-
We have N strings, each of the same length, say K, in a list strs.
If we make a grid or matrix using these strings with one on each line:
We want to find the number of columns in the matrix that are not in lexicographic order.
To be in lexicographic order:
Each character in the column should be equal to or greater than the corresponding character in the previous row.

Procedure:-
Iterate over the columns j.
For each column, iterate over the rows i starting from index 1 (not zero, since each character is compared with the previous row).
Increment the count of unsorted columns every time a character smaller than the character in the previous row is observed.
This figure demonstrates three cases, each with four rows and a single column:
The first two columns are sorted because the characters are in lexicographic order.
The last column is not sorted because the smaller character e comes after g.

Complexity:-
Time Complexity: O(K⋅N)
Space Complexity: O(1)

Code:-
class Solution {
    public int minDeletionSize(String[] strs) {
        int res = 0;
        for (int j = 0; j < strs[0].length(); j++)
            res += isUnsorted(strs, j);
        return res;
    }

    private int isUnsorted(String[] strs, int j) {
        for (int i = 1; i < strs.length; i++)
            if (strs[i].charAt(j) < strs[i - 1].charAt(j))
                return 1;
        return 0;
    }
}
